Typically, people would say that C containers should leave cleanup to the caller, but it is really the TokenList that 
makes sure that there is strict single ownership over every token and its respective heap allocated lexeme string. Looking
at it this way, token_make initially creates the token by allocating memory on the heap for its lexeme string and passing 
the initial ownership to the caller. The moment that the token is passed to list_push_back, the ownership goes to the list,
which has token within TokenNode. The lexeme string is then eventually freed in the token_free() function with 
free(token.lexeme) when list_remove_first or list_destroy diminishes the node. 

If list_push_back fails to allocate the memory for a new node and doesn't call token_free() after on the token, the heap
memory that was allocated for lexeme is then lost (memory leak). In the implementation, the ownership is still kept by 
making sure that list_push_back frees the token whenever it fails to allocate and list_destroy will release any memory
allocated for token during cleanup.
